NNAAMMEE
     mv - move/rename files and directories

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     mmvv [-ff] _o_l_d_f_i_l_e _n_e_w_f_i_l_e
     mmvv [-ff] _f_i_l_e ... _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     mmvv renames files.  In the first form, mmvv changes the name of
     _o_l_d_f_i_l_e to _n_e_w_f_i_l_e. If _n_e_w_f_i_l_e already exists, mmvv replaces
     it with the file being moved; if not, mmvv creates it.  If the
     last argument is a directory, mmvv instead uses the second
     form: each _f_i_l_e is moved so that it resides under that
     _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y, under its own last-component name.

     Normally, mmvv performs the move by creating a link from _n_e_w-
     _f_i_l_e to _o_l_d_f_i_l_e and then removing _o_l_d_f_i_l_e. If that fails be-
     cause _o_l_d_f_i_l_e and _n_e_w_f_i_l_e are on different file systems, mmvv
     instead copies _o_l_d_f_i_l_e to _n_e_w_f_i_l_e (preserving its permission
     mode, and, unless the caller owns _o_l_d_f_i_l_e, clearing the set-
     user-id and set-group-id bits) and then removes _o_l_d_f_i_l_e.

     mmvv will not move a directory across file systems, will not
     rename .  or .., and will not move a directory into one of
     its own descendants.  If _n_e_w_f_i_l_e exists, is not a directory,
     and is unwritable, mmvv refuses to remove it unless -ff is
     given.

     -ff   Force the move: remove an existing, unwritable _n_e_w_f_i_l_e
          instead of refusing to replace it.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     ccpp, llnn

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
     mmvv prints a message and sets a nonzero exit status for each
     _f_i_l_e it cannot move, for example because _o_l_d_f_i_l_e does not
     exist, the caller lacks permission to remove it from its
     parent directory, the destination parent directory does not
     exist or is not writable, or _o_l_d_f_i_l_e and _n_e_w_f_i_l_e are the
     same file.

NNOOTTEESS
     mmvv tests the legality of a directory move using search per-
     mission on the directories from the destination back to the
     root; the superuser always has search permission and so can
     construct a move that disconnects part of the directory
     tree.
